On Feb 9, 7:18 pm, derekt75 <derek.truesd...@anritsu.com> wrote:

> > I'm trying to work out the risk/benefit profile
> > of starting to read spellbooks pretty early,
>
> If I'm >= 85% success rate, I'll read one.

That sounds reasonable.

> High level books simply aren't worthwhile,

Right, I figured that much.  The exact
cutoff depends of course on my intelligence,
but I generally consider level-5 books to be
high level, and level 4 is sort of borderline.

Of course, even mid-level books can be
out of reach in the early game, depending
on XL and Int.

> speaking of intelligence stats, I think not
> lchemizing potions is one of the things I
> find oddest about many nethack players.

I generally don't start alchemy until I get
most of the relevant potions identified and
have enough leisure time to mess with it
without worrying about running out of food.

> to do.  but I just don't understand why people would quaff potions of
> healing when after a few dips, they could instead have a stack of
> blessed full healing.  or blessed gain ability.

I quaff blessed potions of healing on the early levels (say,
before Delphi) on the grounds that the extra max HP
raises my chances of even making it to the point of
being able to identify enough potions to know how to
do alchemy.

I would probably also quaff blessed potions of
healing if they were smoky.

> It seems odd to me to see dumplogs of folks ascending
> with only 14 Int or only 120 HP.

Those could be speed runs, I suppose.

> question is not whether you should alchemize or not.  The question is
> when you want to quaff the blessed full healing and when you want to
> quaff blessed gain ability.

Indeed.  If I'm going to be doing both, I tend to want
to quaff gain ability first and full healing subsequently,
but if I only have enough potions really to do one or
the other (say, in the early middle game when I'm
doing my first alchemy), I sometimes go for full healing,
because max hitpoints really do contribute quite a lot
to survival.  Sometimes the choice hinges on factors
specific to role or game circumstances.

> As to the question of when to start, the answer
> is as soon as you have enough speed and gain
> energy to get what you need.

There are also occasions when I value gain energy too
much to spend it on alchemy.  This definitely depends
on role and other more specific game circumstances.

> To me, the potion of gain energy would be
> better named a potion of alchemy, since I'll
> rarely use one for anything other than alchemy.

It depends.  Some roles start with really low
max energy, and if you don't find any corpse-
dropping newts on the first couple of dungeon
levels (where they have disproportionately high
impact, because they add to a very small
number that later gets multiplied when you
gain levels -- newts while at experience level
1 or 2 are another example of a reason I'll
break vegetarian conduct as a monk) you
can end up in the early middle game with
spells or controlled teleport that you can't
use most of the time.  Quaffing a couple
of blessed potions of gain energy just about
as soon as you get them identified can tip
the balance for your character (especially
as you gain a couple more levels afterward)
so that you can do significant casting.  For
some characters this can be a big deal.
My first NAO ascension was a Valkyrie
that did quite a bit of spellcasting, and
I'm pretty sure she quaffed some potions
of gain energy at some point during the
middle game.

She did also use some potions of gain
energy for alchemy, however.

For me, potions of speed and gain level
are the ones that I almost always use for
alchemy until rather late in the game.
I don't usually quaff potions of gain level
until I've maxed all my attributes, unless
I have a particular need to do the quest
as early as possible (e.g., Tourists gain
huge benefits from their quest artifact).


